Project Brief Title: Simulation Tool for Economic Crisis Prediction 2/2 (Lambda BI Ltd \'95 Cemre Yoldas)\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Application / Codebase Title: UK Economic Simulator (Subtitled in code: "Behavioural-agent macro model \'b7 econometric VAR \'b7 ML predictive suite \'b7 interactive scenario tools")\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 1.2 Purpose\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 The project aims to develop a data-driven simulation and predictive framework that integrates micro-level consumer spending behaviour and expectations with aggregate macroeconomic indicators. It addresses the historical disconnect where macroeconomic forecasting models analyse aggregate variables in isolation from behavioral insights into consumer spending, limiting the ability of analysts and decision-makers to evaluate scenarios during periods of economic instability.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 1.3 Problem Being Addressed\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Economic crises are driven by complex interactions between aggregate macroeconomic indicators (e.g., inflation, interest rates, output growth) and individual-level behavioural responses. Traditional macroeconomic frameworks often overlook or oversimplify these micro-level behavioral dynamics (such as changing consumer sentiment, extrapolative expectations, or heuristic switching). This creates an analytical gap: a lack of flexible, data-driven simulation tools capable of modelling how micro-level behavioural responses interact with macroeconomic dynamics during crisis episodes.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 1.4 Research Question\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Can integrating energy price shock dynamics and micro-founded agent-based behavioural expectations (specifically fundamentalist vs. extrapolative heuristics) into econometric and machine learning pipelines improve out-of-sample predictability and scenario simulation of UK economic output and retail spending during crisis periods?\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
